#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da048bd9-dc9a-50c0-af83-2b20072066fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366260} {:answer/id #uuid "220cddc0-c466-5dbb-b6e3-43bc099f566f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366261} {:answer/id #uuid "60e3e31e-30e9-54b5-9172-49580f7ed8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366262} {:answer/id #uuid "feb24f6d-16b3-581e-804d-ef18536ea554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366263} {:answer/id #uuid "b31d9cec-e44d-5e1d-8ef2-481068b09d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b31d9cec-e44d-5e1d-8ef2-481068b09d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366264} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8850dd-662c-5c77-822a-e1e3e6aa5880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3359bd2-48fb-59d6-ba98-93f115a49404" :question/math-academy-id "q-116501" :question/problem "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{2}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})\\cdot u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{u} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28c9f74a-2f55-5aa7-950e-aa47da69c492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366267} {:answer/id #uuid "4942eb32-f8ea-584f-8871-735413e1a855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366268} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1a8f2d-807f-543c-b853-55b7e69b3536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366269} {:answer/id #uuid "5faf8e07-f4e3-5e39-abcf-3ccb6f01231e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366270} {:answer/id #uuid "736bc42e-57b3-5f50-8aba-6d19a3773b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5faf8e07-f4e3-5e39-abcf-3ccb6f01231e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366270} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6d8105d-5f9f-5b10-b6c1-79aa5aa0078e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f496b800-2ee8-5e43-a695-4b3e8a838990" :question/math-academy-id "q-116504" :question/problem "Determine ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=3x-5$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(3x-5)-1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4d687a7-5b56-51cc-a2e5-1e5dd3769983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366274} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a7aaef-775e-5c38-a90c-3b27c798a832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366275} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8daae6-c46c-59c0-b4bc-632d83530bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366276} {:answer/id #uuid "67fa7e2e-b529-5b31-b2c0-733c60bd4f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366277} {:answer/id #uuid "30a894d7-4c60-578d-8a3c-b50a5ab08cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67fa7e2e-b529-5b31-b2c0-733c60bd4f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366277} :answer-field/id #uuid "24240ad9-c85e-50b8-9a3d-62011c712b64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70a0aba7-0b5b-5702-ba2e-1ffda4c05a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-116548" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and ${3}^{x}+2.$\n\nThe integrand $(6t-7)$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(6x-7)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6u-7) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6({3}^{x}+2)-7) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6\\cdot {3}^{x}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u={3}^{x}+2.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =7-6(0)+({3}^{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6\\cdot {3}^{0}+5) \\\\  & =7+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba97e077-8e4f-5222-86da-2f3f04af30f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076456} {:answer/id #uuid "e53e50b1-1afb-5eca-82ee-0397217f3777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076457} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3103f6-1ff9-5ff7-9e73-bae8670ed685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076458} {:answer/id #uuid "097ee8ac-5fa7-52cb-ac30-7c0c8c6f8734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076459} {:answer/id #uuid "64905089-d174-5f16-9b7d-208e834cbbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64905089-d174-5f16-9b7d-208e834cbbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076460} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b2da7f5-6773-5c30-8225-98a9be26051d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee6d25b7-0717-51a6-88fe-073d54984a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-4718" :question/problem "Find ${F}^{′}(0)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\sqrt[3]{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(0)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\sqrt[3]{1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c6c102-1213-5b92-a66e-a68563ddf4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(2)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366281} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0c9c36-1945-558d-a753-2ab62d123bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(2)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366282} {:answer/id #uuid "ff134327-c31d-5c37-89f5-2c0bce6c3642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(2)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366283} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdebea1-63d9-550e-92b5-6e047eefe076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(2)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366284} {:answer/id #uuid "4255bb9d-f0a7-5348-a382-09a11c6d9490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42c6c102-1213-5b92-a66e-a68563ddf4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(2)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366281} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e7ccd6d-95ff-5bdd-bd86-a85c5563fa5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71788e1c-b47b-5cdc-ab12-b5675e14b728" :question/math-academy-id "q-75221" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-x.$ Find ${g}^{′}(2)$ given the function is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{x}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\int }_{x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{0}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{0}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{0}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 2)-2) \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
